good morning are you worried that the  lights we might have blackouts this  winter I'm very worried about our energy  security I think anyone who's been  following the situation would be worried  I'm also very worried about the fact  that people are struggling to pay their  energy bills right now and they're about  to face eye-watering hikes in mortgage  payments as well we've published  analysis this morning that shows that an  average mortgage holder who's coming off  a fixed term mortgage is facing an  average amount on their bills extra  amount of 500 pounds a month if you put  all these things together could be up to  900 parts of the country like London in  the Southeast how would you fix that the  government has got to reverse the mini  budget this is what has caused huge  instability in the markets it's meant  that mortgage products have been  withdrawn it's meant that mortgage  lenders have been pricing in higher  interest rates and the government this  week in Birmingham doubled down on this  by hinting at more unfunded tax rate tax  cuts to call them unless they reverse  the mini budget we're going to continue  to see this instability although I did  feature a mortgage lender earlier on our  mortgage broker I should say he says  we've sort of reached the Peak at the  moment and he expects that to fall we've  spoken in the last few days to a number  of mortgage lenders and also to the  house builders Federation and others  about what they expect to see they want  to see more stability they want to see a  calming of the markets and in order to  do that the government has got to come  forward and publish the impact of its  proposals and to explain how they would  pay for these billion pounds worth of  cuts to corporation tax and to cutting  the cost of shopping for tourists who  come to the UK billions of pounds spent  on that as well that's the quickest way  that the government could get stability  back into the markets and get certainty  back because otherwise what is going to  happen is that house builders property  developers are going to sit on land  rather than build it because people  can't get mortgages and there's no  Market that's going to worsen the  housing shortage push-up housing prices  and you could find a lot of families in  this country in negative equity you say  stabilize the markets I mean just  looking at the pound against the dollar  there it is stable now but it's it we've  seen a huge fall in the value of the  pound over the last few days but we've  also seen an increase in interest rates  and their forecasts to rise even further  and the problem in relation to mortgages  is mortgages rise ahead of that because  mortgage lenders will price that in at  the moment part of the reason that a lot  of mortgage products have been withdrawn  is because they don't know what rate to  price that in at because they don't know  if there's going to be further  instability caused by more unfunded  borrowing that's why we're calling on  the chancellor to reverse this decision  they have crashed the economy and poured  fuel on a global crisis and working  people are currently paying the price  um we know that the government is  helping as far as energy bills are  concerned should they help with mortgage  bills well there's a very very simple  way that the government could deal with  this and that's to reverse the unfunded  massive package of tax cuts largely  targeted on the wealthiest we think they  should go back to the drawing board and  start again we're calling on the  government to scrap these plans and  start again and next week when  Parliament returns that's what we and I  suspect many back bench story MBS who've  been voicing their concerns at this Tory  conference this week will be calling on  the government to do yeah but that no  you agree with the reversal of the  National Insurance and that's a big part  of the billions of pounds that it's  going to cost well we've opposed that in  Parliament we've voted against it over  and over again because we're we're the  only country in the middle of an  inflation crisis to be putting up taxes  on working people that just seems  completely the wrong set of priorities  we've opposed that separately we'll  continue to oppose it's not that then  what is it corporation tax well we've  we've we opposed the the cut  incorporation tax it seems to me that at  a time when you have to make some  difficult choices choosing to increase  taxes on working people and cut taxes on  the profits of big corporations that's a  government that is just out of touch and  has got completely the wrong set of  priorities so that's about 20 billion or  thereabouts it's not it's it's a drop in  the bucket really isn't it 17 billion  pounds is quite a lot of money  especially when you add to it they've  decided to cut stamp Duty which last  time the former Chancellor housing  market movement well last time the  chancellor Missy sunak  um did that during the covid pandemic it  pushed up house prices because sellers  priced that into the cost of housing and  it cut many more first-time buyers out  of the market so at the opposite effect  that was intended and actually made the  housing crisis worse for a lot of people  who were trying to get into the housing  ladder for the first time this is our  money it's a lot of money and people  haven't got a lot of it at the moment  the government has crashed the economy  working people are paying the price and  that's why we're calling on them to  reverse that decision  do you think emergency call operators  should go on strike I don't but neither  do they and I'm not going to sit here  and condemn emergency call handlers  who've been told that they've got to  choose between staying in a job that  they love and feeding their own families  I will condemn a government that has  crashed the economy and poured fuel on  the fire of rising inflation and caused  interest rates and housing costs arise  for millions of families across the  country and a company that's just given  huge pay Rises to its top brass but is  opening food banks for its own workers  in order to eat they need to pick up the  phone they need to help resolve this  crisis and next week I'll be in  Parliament with colleagues from across  the labor party putting pressure on the  government to get a grip on the economy  and reverse the damage that they've  caused we've seen the conservatives  don't look as United as potentially they  might be when it comes to strikes I mean  you you are not as United as you might  be on the front bench I mean the leader  has told you not to go on the picket  lines and you did I mean I think do that  again I think we're absolutely United  Kirsten has been absolutely clear that  our job is to get into Parliament and  solve this problem get into government  and handle the economy right and that is  exactly what we're focused on that's why  next week I will be in Parliament doing  my job so those emergency call handlers  can get back to work and do their own  I'll always listen to my constituents as  will care as will Angela as will every  member of Labor's front bench but we're  clear and the workforce are clear that  they need us some of the only people in  the country who've got the right to hold  this government to account and summon  them to the House of Commons to do so in  order to solve this crisis for working  people up and down the country but is it  right to defy the leader when he  specifically says don't do it I I  haven't divided the leader I kissed  armor and I from the day that he won  that leadership contest have been on the  same page about what needs to happen in  this country and what our role is to do  it we're going to go out and show that  we're not just an opposition to this  awful Tory government but we're an  alternative we're going to bring back  stability we're not going to put  unfunded huge borrowing cuts to people  and make working people pay the price  for Tory decisions and Tory failure  we've been out fighting for that for two  years and we've been out this last week  at our labor conference showing how we  would get growth back into the economy  by investing in all people and all  places in order to get our economy  working would you go back on the picket  line look I'll always listen to my  constituents but no no Matt but I want  to be absolutely clear about this my job  is to be in Parliament holding this  government no I understand but the  leader said don't go on the picket line  and you did I'm asking you would you do  it again look I'm not I'm not I'm not  you know I'm not going to be on a picket  line next week or in the coming weeks  I'm going to be in Parliament doing my  job and actually I've never gone out and  picketed because that is not my job I've  been a member of parliament now for 12  years I've always met with my  constituents I've always listened to  their concerns and when they go on a  picket line I know that they do it as a  last resort but that's their job my job  as their Member of Parliament has always  been to go out and fight for wider  changes that's what I've always done and  that's what I'll continue to do did he  speak to you about it you know I speak  to Keith Thurman all the time but we've  spoken about the issue about the strikes  about pay demands and about pay  negotiations and we are a hundred  percent on the same page about this that  we're not in government with nobody has  opened the books to us we can't  negotiate in and get involved in  individual disputes but what we can do  is go out and fight for the working  people in this country whether they're  emergency call handlers whether they're  ticket office staff whether they're  nurses who are balloting for strike  action for the first time in over a  hundred years this country is not  working for most people and that's down  to the Tory government who've crashed  our economy and are pouring fuel on the  fire we hear from the chancellor that  drastic action was necessary you know is  a roll of the dice some might say if it  comes off  they're going to win the next election I  mean look at what's happened in the last  couple of weeks within a day of the  chancellor announcing this budget we saw  interest rates expectations Rising by  almost a percentage point and have  continued to rise ever since we've seen  that priced into mortgages so that an  average family coming off a fixed term  mortgage is going to face more than 500  pounds more on their mortgage payments  at a time when food prices are going up  energy bills are going up all that  action on energy bills that was  announced that we supported by the  government just a few weeks ago is going  to be wiped out by the rise in mortgage  payments last time people were paying  this much on their mortgages as a  proportion of their income it sparked  one of the worst housing crises in  British history we can't afford more of  their story government they've got to  reverse these decisions we've got to get  stability back into the markets and  where are you on team Andy Burnham  suggestions that he should move back  into uh Westminster well it's up to Andy  what he decides to do next but he's a  great mate of mine he was my neighbor in  mp and Lee for years and years he's a  great Champion for greater Manchester  and he said the other day that he wants  to continue doing the job the action  said Never Say Never he said Never Say  Never And I think he's right to say  Never Say Never I mean look at what's  just happened in the last couple of  weeks in British politics things change  and things change fast but he is doing a  superb job as the mayor of Greater  Manchester cutting bus fares and helping  young people particularly be able to  move for work and study it's made a  massive difference to the lives of young  people in places like Wigan where buses  matter hugely he's got the right  priorities he's doing a great job and I  want to see him do it for a long time to  come

